 "literal": "Salt merchants having gathered and departed, in the area of the wide place / like a ruined village, the big forest of ōmai trees / 'It is unpleasant,' if you say, / are they pleasant, O Lord, to those who are left alone, the homes?",
 "translation": "If you say that the vast forest of ōmai trees, which looks like a ruined village after the salt merchants have camped and departed, is full of hardships, O Lord, would staying at home be pleasant for her who is left all alone?",
 "modern": "உப்பு வணிகர்கள் தங்கிவிட்டுச் சென்ற பிறகு பாழடைந்து போன ஊரைப் போலத் தோன்றும், ஓமை மரங்கள் நிறைந்த பெரிய காடு துன்பம் தருவது என்று நீங்கள் கூறினால், தலைவரே! உங்களைப் பிரிந்து தனியாக வீட்டில் இருக்கும் தலைவிக்கு மட்டும் அந்த வீடு இன்பம் தருவதாக இருக்குமா?",
 "explanation": "The friend of the heroine speaks to the hero who is hesitating to depart on a journey through the desert to earn wealth. The hero is concerned about the harshness of the desert path, but the friend points out that staying behind is even more painful. She uses the vivid image of a village left desolate after salt merchants have packed up and moved on, comparing it to the vast desert of ōmai trees. This desolation mirrors the state of the heroine's home once the hero leaves. By asking if the home could possibly be pleasant for one left entirely alone, she gently urges him to consider the heroine's feelings. The key words are 'உமணர்' (salt merchants), 'பாழ்த்தன்ன' (like a ruined place), and 'தமியோர்க்கு' (to those who are alone).",
